      <div class="wws-main">
        <div class="wws-head">
          <span class="wws-count">5 Data Solutions</span>
          <h2 class="wws-h">What We Scrape in<br><em>Entertainment &amp; OTT</em></h2>
        </div>
        <div class="wws-topic" id="topic-1">
          <div class="wws-topic-num">01</div>
          <div class="wws-topic-body">
           
            <div class="wws-topic-content">
              <h3 class="wws-topic-t">Content Catalog Extraction</h3>
              <span class="wws-topic-h2">How Entertainment Businesses Use Extracted Catalog Data to Benchmark Content
                Libraries</span>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">A streaming platform's content catalog is its primary asset. Subscribers choose
                services based on what movies and shows are available. Competitors constantly add new titles, remove
                existing ones, and rotate regional libraries. Without systematic extraction, content teams make
                decisions based on incomplete or outdated information. We help entertainment businesses extract content
                catalog data from any streaming platform or entertainment database. Our web extraction pipelines capture
                complete libraries including metadata for every title across multiple regions and platforms.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Our content catalog extraction solutions capture movie data including title,
                original title, release year, runtime, genre classifications, age rating, language, subtitles available,
                and audio options. For series, we extract show title, season numbers, episode titles per season, episode
                runtimes, season release dates, and episode release schedules. We capture additional metadata such as
                director names, cast lists, writers, producers, and production companies. We also extract platform
                specific identifiers, content tags, and content flags such as 4K availability, HDR support, or download
                availability. For example, we can extract the full movie catalog of a competitor platform in a specific
                country to identify which titles are exclusive to them. We can extract all series with a specific genre
                tag like documentary or thriller to analyze depth in that category. We can also extract episodic data
                for ongoing series to track when new episodes are released relative to your own schedule.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">We build custom catalog extraction pipelines based on your target platforms, target
                countries or regions, content types including movies, series, or both, metadata fields needed, and
                extraction frequency from weekly to monthly. Delivery options include content catalog datasets, library
                comparison reports, genre distribution summaries, exclusivity identification lists, and API feeds into
                your content management or competitive intelligence systems. With our extracted data, streaming
                platforms understand exactly what competitors offer. Content acquisition teams identify missing titles
                for their library. Programming teams spot trends in genre investment across the industry. And analysts
                track catalog size and composition changes over time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="wws-topic" id="topic-2">
          <div class="wws-topic-num">02</div>
          <div class="wws-topic-body">
            
            <div class="wws-topic-content">
              <h3 class="wws-topic-t">Metadata &amp; Rating Collection</h3>
              <span class="wws-topic-h2">How Entertainment Businesses Use Extracted Rating Data to Assess Content
                Value</span>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Ratings and metadata drive content decisions. A movie with high IMDb and Rotten
                Tomatoes scores is valuable for acquisition. A series with poor user ratings may need repositioning or
                removal. Critic reviews provide qualitative context. Without systematic extraction, content teams rely
                on manual checks or incomplete data. We help entertainment businesses extract rating and metadata
                information from any streaming platform, review aggregator, or entertainment database. Our web
                extraction pipelines capture numerical scores, rating distributions, critic summaries, and detailed
                content descriptors at scale.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Our metadata and rating collection solutions extract numerical scores including
                IMDb average rating, IMDb vote count, Rotten Tomatoes tomatometer score, Rotten Tomatoes audience score,
                Metacritic score, and platform specific ratings such as Netflix's match percentage or Amazon's star
                rating. We capture rating distributions showing how many users gave each star rating from one to five or
                ten. We extract critic reviews including review text, critic name, publication, and score where
                available. We also extract content descriptors such as parental guides, content warnings, age ratings by
                region including PG, PG 13, R, or TV MA, and thematic tags like violence, language, or adult content.
                For example, we can extract IMDb ratings for all movies in a competitor's catalog to identify highly
                rated titles you may want to acquire. We can extract Rotten Tomatoes audience scores for original series
                across multiple platforms to benchmark your content against industry averages. We can also extract
                parental guidance data for children's content to ensure compliance with regional standards.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">We build custom metadata extraction pipelines based on your target platforms,
                target content titles or catalogs, rating sources needed, critic review depth, and extraction frequency
                from weekly to monthly. Delivery options include rating datasets, score comparison reports, critic
                review summaries, content descriptor exports, and API feeds into your content valuation or acquisition
                systems. With our extracted data, content acquisition teams prioritize high rated titles for licensing.
                Programming teams identify underperforming content for removal or promotion. Parents and compliance
                teams access age rating data across multiple regional standards. And analysts correlate ratings with
                viewership to refine content value models.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="wws-topic" id="topic-3">
          <div class="wws-topic-num">03</div>
          <div class="wws-topic-body">
           
            <div class="wws-topic-content">
              <h3 class="wws-topic-t">User Review &amp; Comment Extraction</h3>
              <span class="wws-topic-h2">How Entertainment Businesses Use Extracted User Reviews to Understand Audience
                Sentiment</span>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">User reviews reveal what audiences truly think. A high average rating may hide
                widespread criticism of specific elements like pacing, acting, or plot holes. A low average rating may
                come from review bombing rather than genuine poor quality. Reading individual reviews at scale is
                impossible. We help entertainment businesses extract user review and comment data from any streaming
                platform, review aggregator, or entertainment database. Our web extraction pipelines capture structured
                review data including text, ratings, metadata, and user information for thousands of titles.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Our user review extraction solutions capture review text including the full written
                content, review title or headline, and any spoiler warnings. We extract numeric ratings such as star
                ratings from one to five or ten point scores. We capture engagement metrics including helpfulness votes,
                like counts, reply counts, and report counts. We extract timestamp data including review date, last
                edited date, and platform specific indicators such as verified purchase or verified watch. We also
                capture user profile information where publicly available including username, user review count, user
                helpfulness score, and join date. For example, we can extract all user reviews for a specific original
                series across multiple platforms to compare sentiment between services. We can identify reviews with
                high helpfulness votes to surface the most valued audience opinions. We can also track how review
                sentiment for a title changes after a controversial episode or casting announcement.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">We build custom review extraction pipelines based on your target platforms, target
                content titles, minimum review rating or length filters, date ranges, and extraction frequency from
                daily to weekly. Delivery options include review datasets, sentiment summaries by episode or season,
                helpful review identification lists, review trend reports, and API feeds into your content analytics or
                audience research systems. With our extracted data, content teams identify specific audience complaints
                to address in future productions. Marketing teams find authentic positive review quotes for campaigns.
                Competitive intelligence teams compare audience reception of competing titles. And researchers analyze
                how review patterns differ across platforms and demographics.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="wws-topic" id="topic-4">
          <div class="wws-topic-num">04</div>
          <div class="wws-topic-body">
            
            <div class="wws-topic-content">
              <h3 class="wws-topic-t">Pricing &amp; Subscription Plan Monitoring</h3>
              <span class="wws-topic-h2">How Entertainment Businesses Use Extracted Pricing Data to Optimize
                Subscription Strategy</span>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Pricing is a critical lever in the streaming wars. A platform that raises prices
                may lose subscribers to cheaper competitors. A platform that introduces an ad supported tier may capture
                price sensitive customers. Regional price differences create arbitrage and cross border subscription
                shifts. Without systematic extraction, pricing teams react to competitor changes weeks or months late.
                We help entertainment businesses extract pricing and subscription data from any streaming platform. Our
                web extraction pipelines capture tiered pricing, promotional offers, free trial terms, and regional
                variations across multiple countries.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Our pricing and subscription plan extraction solutions capture subscription tier
                details including tier names such as basic, standard, premium, or ad supported. We extract monthly
                prices, annual prices, and any discounted prepaid plans like quarterly or half yearly. We capture free
                trial information including trial duration, credit card requirement, and auto renewal terms. We extract
                promotional pricing such as first month discounts, student pricing, bundled offers with other services,
                and telecom carrier partnerships. We also capture regional price variations for the same platform across
                different countries, accounting for currency and purchasing power differences. For example, we can
                extract all pricing tiers for competitor platforms in your target markets to see where your pricing sits
                relative to the market. We can track price change history over time to understand competitor willingness
                to raise or lower prices. We can also extract promotional offers and limited time discounts to see which
                customer segments competitors are targeting.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">We build custom pricing extraction pipelines based on your target platforms, target
                countries or regions, subscription tiers, promotional offer tracking, and extraction frequency from
                weekly to monthly or immediately upon price change detection. Delivery options include pricing
                comparison datasets, price change alert reports, regional variation matrices, promotional offer
                trackers, and API feeds into your pricing strategy or competitive intelligence systems. With our
                extracted data, pricing teams adjust subscription fees based on real competitor positioning. Marketing
                teams design promotional offers that beat competitor discounts. Finance teams model subscriber response
                to price changes using historical competitor data. And strategy teams identify markets where competitors
                are overpriced or underpriced.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="wws-topic" id="topic-5">
          <div class="wws-topic-num">05</div>
          <div class="wws-topic-body">
            
            <div class="wws-topic-content">
              <h3 class="wws-topic-t">New Arrival &amp; Leaving Soon Alerts</h3>
              <span class="wws-topic-h2">How Entertainment Businesses Use Arrival and Departure Data to Stay Ahead of
                Library Shifts</span>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Streaming libraries are not static. Titles arrive every week. Titles leave every
                month. A movie that moves from one platform to another can shift subscriber preference overnight. A
                series that leaves without warning creates subscriber frustration. Tracking these changes across
                multiple platforms and regions is impossible manually. We help entertainment businesses extract new
                arrival and leaving soon data from any streaming platform. Our web extraction pipelines capture coming
                soon announcements, weekly release schedules, and expiration notices at the regional level.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">Our new arrival and leaving soon extraction solutions capture incoming title data
                including movie and series titles, arrival dates, episode drop schedules for weekly releases, and season
                premiere dates. We extract content metadata for new arrivals including genres, runtime, cast, language,
                and age rating. For leaving titles, we capture departure dates, last watch notices, and platform
                specific expiration flags. We also track regional differences in arrival and departure dates, as a title
                may leave in one country but remain in another. For example, we can extract all new arrivals across
                competitor platforms for the next 30 days to identify content gaps in your own upcoming schedule. We can
                track leaving soon titles to alert subscribers before favorites disappear, reducing churn risk. We can
                also identify titles moving from one platform to another, revealing licensing shifts and potential
                acquisition targets.</p>
              <p class="wws-topic-p">We build custom arrival and departure extraction pipelines based on your target
                platforms, target countries or regions, content types including movies or series, time horizon such as
                next 7, 30, or 90 days, and extraction frequency from daily to weekly. Delivery options include new
                arrival datasets, leaving soon alerts, release calendar exports, regional difference reports, and API
                feeds into your content planning or subscriber communication systems. With our extracted data, streaming
                platforms benchmark their upcoming content against competitors. Marketing teams promote new arrivals
                before competitors announce similar titles. Retention teams warn subscribers about leaving favorites and
                suggest alternatives. And content acquisition teams identify high value titles moving between services.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

